.TH qsh-attach 1  "attach 0.5.8" 
.SH NAME
qsh\-attach \- Re\-attach this terminal to a session that is already running
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBqsh attach\fR [\fB\-\-escape\-char\fR] [\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-\-jsonl\fR] [\fB\-\-screen\-sync\fR] [\fB\-v\fR|\fB\-\-verbose\fR]... [\fB\-q\fR|\fB\-\-quiet\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-ssh\fR] [\fB\-\-install\-remote\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-install\-remote\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] [\fB\-V\fR|\fB\-\-version\fR] <\fISESSION_REF\fR> 
.SH DESCRIPTION
Re\-attach this terminal to a session that is already running.
.PP
Only possible from the device that opened the session, whose resume credential is bound to it (`docs/CLI.md` §6.2, §7). Detach with the escape sequence `~d`; the session keeps running.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-escape\-char\fR \fI<CHAR>\fR
Escape character for the line\-start detach sequences, or `none`. Default `~`; only active when stdin is a terminal
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
Emit a single `qsh.cli/v1` JSON envelope on stdout instead of human\-readable text
.TP
\fB\-\-jsonl\fR
Emit newline\-delimited JSON on stdout (reserved for streaming commands; non\-streaming commands emit a single line, same as `\-\-json`)
.TP
\fB\-\-screen\-sync\fR \fI<MODE>\fR
Screen state sync: `auto` (default), `on` (require it) or `off` (plain byte stream). See `qsh \-\-help`
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Increase diagnostic verbosity on stderr (`\-v` info, `\-vv` debug, `\-vvv` trace). Never affects stdout (`docs/CLI.md` §2.2)
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
Suppress diagnostic output on stderr
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-ssh\fR
Never run ssh on this command\*(Aqs behalf: no automatic bootstrap of an unknown name, and no restarting of a pinned host\*(Aqs serve over ssh when the connection fails (`docs/CLI.md` §7). `qsh pair ssh` is not affected; it is ssh by definition
.TP
\fB\-\-install\-remote\fR
When the ssh bootstrap finds no `qsh` on the remote host (or one too old to answer it), install this machine\*(Aqs release into `~/.local/bin` there without asking (`docs/CLI.md` §7). Works with `\-\-json`
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-install\-remote\fR
Never install or upgrade `qsh` on the remote host, and do not ask; a missing `qsh` ends as `remote_qsh_missing`
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
.TP
\fB\-V\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
Print version
.TP
<\fISESSION_REF\fR>
Opaque session handle (`<host>/<session_id>`) from `session open` or `sessions`
.SH VERSION
v0.5.8
